  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of today through next Saturday...\n\nWesterly flow aloft continues across our area thanks to a low \namplitude ridge that persists across the entire region. A weak \nimpulse in the flow was producing some isolated thundershowers \nearlier over east central Montana this morning. This energy and \nassociated nose of an upper jet-let stretched across our CWA will \nprovide a trigger for convection in our far eastern counties \n(Fallon,Carter) later today. MUCAPE near 1000 J/kg in the \nMT/Dakota border region with 0-6 km shear around 35 kts suggest a \nrisk of strong to marginally severe thunderstorms possible in this\narea. SPC has the SE corner of MT in a marginal severe risk \noutlook which seems reasonable. PWATs over an inch suggest heavy \ndownpours and local flood issues are possible as well with \nstronger cells. In the meantime, another weak disturbance in the \nflow will help initiate convection over our western \nzones/mountains late today with isolated to scattered \nshowers/storms moving off the higher terrain into the nearby \nfoothills and plains this evening. CAMs are inconclusive how far \neast this activity may get before weakening, but recent evenings \nconvection has not been robust enough to affect even Yellowstone \ncounty. We will leave a slight chance in there for the central \nzones past sunset. \n\nMonday into Tuesday...model ensembles agree that shortwave energy \nwill push through NE MT with a trailing cold front into east\ncentral Montana. This along with associated upper jet energy \naloft looks to produce enough frontogenesis and lift over our \nregion for periods of scattered showers and thunderstorms each \nday. Models do differ on position of the upper jet, which in turn \ndetermines placement of better rainfall in our CWA. For now...it \nappears the higher terrain along the MT/WY border has the best \npotential of meaningful precipitation. PWATs are progged to be \n0.8-1.0 inches, which suggests locally heavy rain will need to \nmonitored, especially over old burn scars. \n\nFor the last half of the work week, ensembles indicate confidence \nin a strengthening ridge over the inter-mountain west. This should\nresult in a warming trend back into the lower 90s by Friday with \na much diminished chance of rainfall. BT",
